<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Crispi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Crispi was an Italian statesman, a Sicilian veteran of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s Expedition of the Thousand, who was prime minister in 1887–1891 and 1893–1896. He pursued a strong executive, close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and colonial expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and harshly suppressed the Sicilian Fasci movement. He fell from power after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi.jpg" alt="Francesco Crispi" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Crispi_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesco Crispi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> on 4 October 1818 into a family of Italo-Albanian origin. His paternal family came from Palazzo Adriano, a community founded in the later fifteenth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> or Greek Catholic Albanians, the Arbëreshë, and his grandfather was an Arbëreshë Orthodox priest. In December 1847 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> to prepare the revolution against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies</a>, which began on 12 January 1848, the king's birthday.</p><p>After it failed he went into exile, first in Marseille, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, where he worked as a journalist and befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>, and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in June 1859, and in 1860 he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> and became First Secretary of State in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s provisional government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected in 1861 for the Historical Left, Crispi kept his seat in every legislature until his death. He was known as the most aggressive member of his group, and in 1864 he abandoned Mazzini for the monarchy, writing that the monarchy united them and the republic would divide them. He became President of the Chamber of Deputies in 1876 and, in December 1877, Minister of the Interior, but was forced to resign in March 1878 after being accused of bigamy. In 1883 he opposed the coalition politics known as Trasformismo and founded the Dissident Left, called the Pentarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Crispi became prime minister on 29 July 1887, the first from Southern Italy, and in his first term also served as foreign minister and interior minister. With Giuseppe Zanardelli he abolished the death penalty, repealed anti-strike laws and reformed the penal code and public health, and he strengthened the role of the prime minister. He saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the permanent enemy, visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a> to consult on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a>, and turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. He resigned on 6 February 1891 after a budget deficit cost him his majority.</p><p>He returned in December 1893, amid disturbances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the Banca Romana scandal. The Fasci Siciliani, a democratic and socialist movement of poor workers, were suppressed under martial law, and after an anarchist tried to shoot him on 16 June 1894 he passed anti-anarchist laws that were also used against socialists. Italian forces beat the Mahdists at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agordat">Agordat</a> in December 1893 and occupied Kassala in 1894. His second term ended on 14 June 1896 after the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a>, and he died on 11 August 1901.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco Crispi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Francesco Crispi</b> pursued a strong state and colonial expansion in the 1890s, and Giovanni Giolitti dominated the years 1901–1914 with social reforms and accommodation of moderate socialists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ribera, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.5,13.27&amp;year=1871" aria-label="Ribera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francesco Crispi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5892 4309h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5892 4309h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.5,13.27&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
